WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.389
Speaker 1: Well, it happens every once in
a while.

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:05.650
At the last minute, an interview goes wrong.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.520
And I don't mean wrong in the sense that yesterday's
urine injector interview went wrong.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.031
I mean, like, the interview just doesn't happen
because of a problem.

00:00:14.031 --> 00:00:15.360
So we're going to take a few calls.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.560
Let's hear from a few people in the audience.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.500
Rare that we're able to do this on a Wednesday,
but let's see what we get.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.500
Okay.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.170
Why don't we start first with Jay from Ontario.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.400
Jay from Ontario, welcome to the program.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.590
Speaker 3: Hello?

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.390
Speaker 1: Yes, You're on the air.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:39.960
Speaker 2: I was just curious with the all
the news about Trump and whatever the other

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.030
guy's name is.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.290
Giuliani, rather, selling pardons for 2 million.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:50.170
I wonder if it's in the best interest of Democrats
to try to pass a bunch of laws not allowing

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.879
presidents to have so much power.

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:54.790
And I know that that could be a wide range
of things.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.550
But I wanted to know your thoughts on that,
if the Democrats should try to do that.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:05.091
Speaker 1: Well, I mean, sure, the problem
is that presidential pardon power is not a

00:01:05.091 --> 00:01:07.380
likely thing to be changed.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.770
And so then the question becomes, what exactly
are you trying to take away as far as powers

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:12.770
go?

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.409
Now, here's here's what I will say, Jay.

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:21.580
One of the realities that the Trump presidency
exposed is that our safeguards and checks

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.549
and balances aren't as robust and impenetrable
as maybe the framers of the Constitution believe

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:28.939
them to be.

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:35.880
And in the defense of the framers, they probably
reasonably never believed someone as willing

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.000
to flout all of those checks and balances
as Trump would come to power.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.510
Now, you could say, well, they should have
foreseen that.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.130
Okay, but they didn't.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.380
So then the question becomes, what exactly
is it that you would want to change?

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.130
I don't know if you have something in mind.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.860
Speaker 2: No, I don't.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.330
And I think that's the big problem, is that
the Trump presidency exposed that there's

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.860
probably a lot of things that we do need to
worry about that we never thought that we

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.860
would have to.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.860
Speaker 1: Absolutely.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.880
And when it comes to pardon, you know, the
crazy thing, as we talked about earlier in

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.299
the week, it's not explicit.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:11.461
It appears not to be explicitly illegal for
the president to sell pardons, except if it

00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:15.120
counts as a bribe or as a fraudulent or obstructive
act.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.610
And, you know, to be totally honest, I don't
think this selling pardons for 2 million thing

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:21.910
is going to go very far.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.750
I hope to be wrong and I hope to eat my hat
on it, even though I don't wear hats.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.760
But I actually don't think it's going to go
very far.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.100
Speaker 2: Yeah, I guess I agree.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.120
Speaker 1: You calling from Ontario, Canada?

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.140
Speaker 3: Yes.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.150
Speaker 1: Beautiful.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.150
How is it there?

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.989
Speaker 2: Uh, no complaints.

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:39.519
Well, it's pretty good, I guess.

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:40.519
Speaker 1: That's good.

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:41.560
All right, Jay, thank you for the call.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.140
Speaker 3: Yeah, Thanks for having me on,
David.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.140
Speaker 1: Take care.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.140
Right.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.170
There goes Jay from Ontario calling in very
strongly.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.150
Let's go to Liam from Austin, Texas.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:57.200
Liam, are you thrilled to have Elon Musk in
Austin these days or what?

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.659
Speaker 3: I'm sorry.

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:00.849
Could you repeat the question, please?

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:02.209
Speaker 1: I was joking.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.390
Whether you were glad that Elon Musk is in
your neck of the woods.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.489
Speaker 3: Oh, yeah.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:08.540
Not particularly.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.710
You know, I'm coming to Austin.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.920
Yeah, I'm in Austin for college, so it's really
weird.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.420
You know, campus is not the same as the city
where it feels a lot more like San Francisco.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.420
Right.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.420
Speaker 1: Well, what's on your mind today.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.970
Speaker 3: David?

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.760
This is more of a broad question, but it's
addressed towards you specifically.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.739
So of many of the political pundits that I
follow, you come across to me as one of the.

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:34.209
Speaker 2: Most eloquent, well-spoken.

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:36.489
Speaker 3: Articulated.

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:38.330
And I just I wanted to ask you.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.390
I know I think you majored in communications.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.459
If I did my research right.

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:45.269
I just want to know, how did you get to that
point?

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:49.879
Because, you know, I really want to be a good
public speaker, but I feel like I'm just not,

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:50.879
you know.

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:52.489
Speaker 1: So a couple different things.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:56.640
First of all, you could have even included
a little more praise, really.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.079
Liam, I don't like that you cut it off so
early.

00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:00.560
We could have done with more of it.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.560
No, I'm kidding.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.560
No.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.560
So here's the thing.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.560
There's a lot of different things.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:11.120
I do think that there is some predisposition
to people who are more or less comfortable

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.120
talking.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.040
And I. I just think it's an open question.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.489
It's to the degree to which one can kind of
like learn to be comfortable talking.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:22.070
But I think it's a bunch of different things.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:26.350
First of all, I learned English as a second
language.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:30.260
It's a native language in the sense that I
started learning it when I was five.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:37.590
But I do think that the process of translating
what you know in one language to a new language

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.190
probably helps language development to some
degree.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:47.910
I read a ton since I was a kid and I think
reading is probably the best way to build

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:54.380
vocabulary and to experience different sorts
of sentence structure and this sort of thing.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.199
I think that working in sales was hugely helpful.

00:04:58.199 --> 00:05:04.229
I've told my Circuit City War stories, you
know, being 16 and being put in a position

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:10.930
of selling $3,000 laptops to, you know, 45
year olds, bringing their kid to college or

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:16.860
whatever, it kind of forces you to either
sink or swim and develop rapport and comfort

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.300
thinking on your feet and that sort of thing.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.280
And then I think just by like doing a show.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.310
So I don't know that there's really an answer
in there.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.350
But I think all of those things, you know,
I've attended, I've been invited to attend

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.800
sometimes like Toastmasters is a public speaking
group where people take turns speaking and

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.139
stuff like that.

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:37.230
I don't know to what degree stuff like that
is useful, but maybe it is.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.060
But those are a few things that come to mind.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.590
Speaker 3: Yeah, this is very helpful.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:42.590
Now, do you think?

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:46.930
Just as a quick follow up, do you think that
you were kind of always, since you were, you

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.710
can remember, naturally predisposed towards
speaking publicly?

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.389
You're very comfortable with yourself like
that, because I've always felt kind of like

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:54.389
an anxious person.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:59.740
Speaker 1: No, I mean, to be honest, when
I do in-person stuff, it's like, you know,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:06.190
we get one and a half million views a day
on YouTube and 30, 40,000 podcast downloads

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:07.650
a day.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.590
And it's just second nature at this point
because I'm so used to it.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:17.309
However, if I'm speaking to a room in person
of 50 people, I still get sort of nervous.

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:18.850
So I don't I don't think that goes away.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.280
And I've spoken to people who speak to really
large groups in person.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.520
They all have said to me, If you don't get
a little anxious before that, then then something

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.660
actually might be wrong.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.840
That's actually a good thing as a normal thing.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.690
Obviously, if it becomes disabling, it's a
problem.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.060
Speaker 3: Right.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.060
Well, David.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.400
Sir, I greatly.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.400
Tears in my eyes.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.290
I just so much appreciate your time today.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:07:30.430
Speaker 1: Well, Liam, I'm sorry I made you
cry, but I do appreciate 

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:31.690
the call.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:36.199
Speaker 3: All right.

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:40.710
Speaker 1: Thank you.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:08:05.240
There 

00:08:05.240 --> 00:10:54.490
is Liam from Austin.

